{
  "name": "BlockUnapprovedRMM",
  "label": "Block unapproved RMM and remote access tools",
  "cat": "Intune Standards",
  "tag": [],
  "impact": "High Impact",
  "helpText": "Blocks RMM and remote access tools you have not approved. Windows devices get an App Control for Business policy that stops the tools from starting, Macs get a policy that denies the tools by code signature. Select every tool you use yourself first, your own RMM included, or it is blocked as well. The policies are enforced, so assign them to a pilot group before all devices. Based on RMM Guard (rmmguard.com) by Lewis Barry and Roy Klooster.",
  "executiveText": "Stops attackers from using remote control software to get into company devices by blocking every remote management tool the company has not approved.",
  "docsDescription": "Attackers install a legitimate remote management tool shortly after they get in, because it gives them file transfer, scripting and unattended access without malware. This standard blocks the known tools except the ones you select. On Windows it deploys an enforced App Control for Business policy named 'CIPP - Block unapproved RMM tools (Windows)' that denies each tool by file name and folder. It needs Windows 11 24H2 or newer, because the rules use wildcards older versions do not support. Rules match names and paths, not signatures, so a renamed copy can get past it. On macOS it deploys a settings catalog policy named 'CIPP - Block unapproved RMM tools (macOS)' that denies each tool by the Team ID of its code signature. That needs macOS 27 or newer on a supervised Mac, has no audit mode and only covers the tools with a verified Mac signature. A rule shared by two tools is removed when either of them is allowed. The tool list ships with CIPP, and the policies are reported as out of date and redeployed when a CIPP update brings a newer list. Network blocking with Defender indicators is not part of this standard. The tool list and the approach come from RMM Guard by Lewis Barry and Roy Klooster (https://rmmguard.com, https://github.com/Lewis-Barry/RMM-Blocker), with much of the underlying data from the LOLRMM project (https://lolrmm.io).",
  "impactColour": "danger",
  "addedDate": "2026-11-16",
  "powershellEquivalent": "Graph: POST or PUT deviceManagement/configurationPolicies",
  "recommendedBy": [],
  "requiredCapabilities": [
    "INTUNE_A",
    "MDM_Services",
    "EMS",
    "SCCM",
    "MICROSOFTINTUNEPLAN1"
  ],
  "secureScoreImpact": 0,
  "compare": "subset",
